1
00:00:00,023 --> 00:00:01,593
Real users double-click,

2
00:00:01,593 --> 00:00:02,743
like ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta.

3
00:00:02,997 --> 00:00:03,847
They click a lot.

4
00:00:03,847 --> 00:00:05,807
Real users open different tabs.

5
00:00:05,807 --> 00:00:08,147
Real users network drop halfway.

6
00:00:08,147 --> 00:00:09,287
Real users

7
00:00:09,287 --> 00:00:11,447
leave the page open for two hours,

8
00:00:11,447 --> 00:00:13,247
and they go and then they come back.

9
00:00:13,247 --> 00:00:14,587
And they're not doing anything strange.

10
00:00:14,587 --> 00:00:16,420
They are just being users.

11
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:17,860
someone is going to report an issue.

12
00:00:17,860 --> 00:00:19,343
And it's going to be

13
00:00:19,343 --> 00:00:21,413
exactly that edge case that you never thought

14
00:00:21,413 --> 00:00:22,533
of.

15
00:00:22,963 --> 00:00:24,513
One of the most common edge cases in

16
00:00:24,513 --> 00:00:25,133
production

17
00:00:25,133 --> 00:00:26,833
is the duplicate submission.

18
00:00:26,833 --> 00:00:27,553
If, for example,

19
00:00:27,553 --> 00:00:28,653
in an online shop,

20
00:00:28,653 --> 00:00:30,613
someone clicks place order,

21
00:00:30,613 --> 00:00:32,053
and maybe the page

22
00:00:32,053 --> 00:00:33,133
feels a little slow,

23
00:00:33,133 --> 00:00:34,433
they click it again.

24
00:00:34,433 --> 00:00:36,453
Now our server gets

25
00:00:36,453 --> 00:00:37,053
two requests,

26
00:00:37,053 --> 00:00:38,953
and it creates two orders.

27
00:00:38,953 --> 00:00:40,513
And if we charge on each order,

28
00:00:40,513 --> 00:00:42,553
you can charge the user twice.

29
00:00:42,673 --> 00:00:44,553
Another common edge case in production is a

30
00:00:44,553 --> 00:00:45,233
race condition.

31
00:00:45,233 --> 00:00:46,913
A race condition is when two things happen at

32
00:00:46,913 --> 00:00:47,773
the same time.

33
00:00:47,773 --> 00:00:49,683
And your code just assume that they would

34
00:00:49,683 --> 00:00:51,217
happen one after the other.

35
00:00:51,217 --> 00:00:51,787
For example,

36
00:00:51,787 --> 00:00:53,237
in our online shop,

37
00:00:53,237 --> 00:00:56,020
say we have one item left in stock,

38
00:00:56,020 --> 00:00:59,260
two people click buy in the exact same second,

39
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:01,430
both requests check the stock,

40
00:01:01,430 --> 00:01:03,040
both see one item left,

41
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:04,530
both create an order,

42
00:01:04,530 --> 00:01:06,120
and now we have sold an item that we

43
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:07,540
do not have.

44
00:01:08,060 --> 00:01:08,780
In 2016,

45
00:01:08,780 --> 00:01:10,880
the BBC wrote about a woman called Jennifer

46
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:11,673
Null.

47
00:01:11,673 --> 00:01:13,673
When Jennifer Null tries to buy a plane

48
00:01:13,673 --> 00:01:14,883
ticket, most websites

49
00:01:14,883 --> 00:01:17,143
tell her that she has left the surname field

50
00:01:17,143 --> 00:01:17,853
blank.

51
00:01:17,853 --> 00:01:19,173
Her surname is Null.

52
00:01:19,173 --> 00:01:21,283
The website sees the name Null and treats it

53
00:01:21,283 --> 00:01:22,963
as empty.

54
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:25,520
Another edge case that can cause issues:

55
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:27,233
time zones and daylight saving.

56
00:01:27,233 --> 00:01:28,843
On the 29th of March,

57
00:01:28,843 --> 00:01:31,263
the clocks in Tallinn jump from 3 a.m.

58
00:01:31,263 --> 00:01:32,133
straight to 4 a.m.

59
00:01:32,133 --> 00:01:33,513
So 3:30

60
00:01:33,513 --> 00:01:34,833
that morning never happened.

61
00:01:34,833 --> 00:01:37,473
Now imagine our online shop sends a daily

62
00:01:37,473 --> 00:01:38,770
sales report at 3:30,

63
00:01:38,770 --> 00:01:40,293
Tallinn time.

64
00:01:40,293 --> 00:01:41,893
On one day of the year,

65
00:01:41,893 --> 00:01:42,503
that

66
00:01:42,503 --> 00:01:43,573
job would not run.

67
00:01:43,573 --> 00:01:45,713
You can't protect your app from every single

68
00:01:45,713 --> 00:01:46,913
possible edge case

69
00:01:46,913 --> 00:01:48,483
because you can't even predict every single

70
00:01:48,483 --> 00:01:49,993
one. If you want to wait till you predict

71
00:01:49,993 --> 00:01:51,283
every single one,

72
00:01:51,283 --> 00:01:52,633
you probably wouldn't ship anything.

73
00:01:52,633 --> 00:01:54,433
What do we do about this?
